<h2 class="ds-section-title">The two of us</h2>

<p class="about-lede">
  Between us, forty years of writing software and running the teams that write it, in corporations, banks, family
  businesses and early-stage startups. We have shipped in languages that no longer have a community, and in ones that
  did not exist when we started.
</p>

<div class="about-stack">
  <span class="about-stack-label">What we write today</span>
  <p>
    Go, Kotlin and TypeScript for the products. SQL for the data, on Postgres, MySQL and ClickHouse. OpenTofu, Nix and
    Kubernetes for the machines that run it all.
  </p>
  <p>
    The list is short on purpose. We would rather be honest about the four things we run in production than claim twenty
    from a CV.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="person-grid">
  <article class="person-card">
    <span class="person-initials">LP</span>
    <h3 class="person-name">Luca Pette</h3>
    <p class="person-role">Co-founder</p>
    <p>
      Luca has run engineering in corporations, family businesses and early-stage startups. He was CTO at Marley Spoon,
      where the product team grew from ten to twenty-five, and Head of Engineering at Airy, where he designed the
      messaging platform, led the migration and open-sourced it. He has been a fractional CTO since 2022, and was an
      independent consultant before that, including a stint as interim CTO at Grover. He is most at home in
      infrastructure, data and backend systems.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article class="person-card">
    <span class="person-initials">SZ</span>
    <h3 class="person-name">Stefano Zanella</h3>
    <p class="person-role">Co-founder</p>
    <p>
      Stefano has spent twenty years building platforms and the teams around them. He built Marley Spoon's platform from
      the ground up as a software developer, then led it as Head of Software Engineering while scaling five teams. He
      grew that platform from a Rails MVP into a Postgres, Beanstalkd, Chef and Ansible architecture, which is where the
      operations half of this company comes from. He was an engineering manager at solarisBank, and Head of Engineering
      at trana and Acto. He works on lean software engineering, delivery measurement, team design and coaching: the
      parts of engineering that decide whether a team of five ships like a team of twenty.
    </p>
  </article>
</div>

<h2 class="ds-section-title">How we work</h2>

<div class="ds-features-grid">
  <div class="ds-feature-card">
    <h3>Two people, no handoffs</h3>
    <p>The person you talk to is the person writing the code. There is nobody else to ask.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="ds-feature-card">
    <h3>Agents write, we read</h3>
    <p>
      We do all our coding through agents and keep the reading. Most of the work is the rules and the loop that make the
      output reviewable.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="ds-feature-card">
    <h3>We build our own tools</h3>
    <p>Our CI, our time tracking, our release process and the licensing server that sells Guido are all ours.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="ds-feature-card">
    <h3>We measure</h3>
    <p>
      Every number we publish comes from our own repository, our own CI or our own bill. Read the
      <a class="about-card-link" href="/writing/how-coding-agents-work/">series on coding agents</a> for the long
      version.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="ds-section-title">The portfolio</h2>

<div class="about-prose">
  <p>
    Six products, from a race engineer on your phone to a Kubernetes scanner, plus the
    <a href="/vetrina/">open-source tools</a> we run the company on. Two are shipped, one is in beta, three are on the
    way.
  </p>
  <p>
    The range looks like a lack of focus. It is the opposite: we pick small problems we have felt ourselves, and we
    solve them well, whichever field they come from.
  </p>
</div>

